The bug is in:

`org.apache.commons.math.stat.descriptive.SummaryStatistics.addValue(double)`

Diagnosis:
- The failing tests override `meanImpl`, `geoMeanImpl`, and `varianceImpl` with fresh instances of `Mean`, `GeometricMean`, and `Variance(false)`.
- In `addValue(double)`, the code only increments the overridden statistic if it is **not** an instance of the corresponding math class:

```java
if (!(meanImpl instanceof Mean)) {
    meanImpl.increment(value);
}
if (!(varianceImpl instanceof Variance)) {
    varianceImpl.increment(value);
}
if (!(geoMeanImpl instanceof GeometricMean)) {
    geoMeanImpl.increment(value);
}
```

- This logic is wrong for the override scenario in the tests. After:
  - `setMeanImpl(new Mean())`
  - `setGeoMeanImpl(new GeometricMean())`
  - `setVarianceImpl(new Variance(false))`

  the fields are overridden, but because they are still instances of `Mean` / `GeometricMean` / `Variance`, `addValue` does **not** increment them.

- As a result:
  - overridden `Mean` keeps its internal `FirstMoment` at `n = 0`, so `getMean()` returns `NaN`
  - overridden `GeometricMean` keeps `SumOfLogs.n = 0`, so `getGeometricMean()` returns `NaN`
  - overridden `Variance(false)` keeps `SecondMoment.n = 0`, so `getVariance()` returns `NaN`

Why this is the root cause:
- The default `SummaryStatistics` wiring relies on shared internal moments, so built-in instances may not need explicit incrementing.
- But once a client replaces one of these implementations with a new statistic object, `SummaryStatistics` must update that exact object.
- The code incorrectly uses `instanceof` as a proxy for “default internal implementation” vs “user-supplied override.” These are not equivalent.

So the method is buggy because it skips updates for overridden implementations whenever they are of the same class as the default implementation.

